{"ledger_line": 11, "event_id": "evt-000011-b1dcc7a30247", "offset": 0, "payload_characters": 25632, "next_offset": 3692, "text": "{\"cwd\":\"[workspace]\",\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"last_assistant_message\":\"## Chapter One: The Clock That Kept the Wrong Promise\\n\\nAt six each evening, the observatory sent a pulse down the hill, and the town\u2019s clocks advanced together with a sound like a hundred people clearing their throats. The railway clock clicked above the booking window. The school clock shivered behind its glass. In the harbor office, a little brass flag dropped so the tide recorder could mark the hour.\\n\\nFor eleven years, Mara had watched the pulse leave through a green lamp above her workbench.\\n\\nOn the morning the trouble began, the lamp flashed while the observatory\u2019s second hand was still approaching the mark.\\n\\nShe put down her tea.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s early,\u201d she said.\\n\\nHer apprentice, Ivo, looked up from a drawer of binding posts. \u201cHow early?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEarly enough to see. Which means we should have noticed before today.\u201d\\n\\nThe clock occupied a cabinet taller than either of them. Its pendulum swung inside a sealed chamber, protected from drafts, while a row of electrical contacts translated its patient motion into signals for the town. Beneath the cabinet, a faded card identified the spare contact assembly.\\n\\nOBSERVATORY KEY: H7Q2M9RX\\n\\nIvo reached for the rate screw.\\n\\nMara caught his wrist.\\n\\n\u201cFirst we find out whether the clock is wrong, the pulse is wrong, or we\u2019re wrong.\u201d\\n\\nHe withdrew his hand with the expression he wore whenever she turned an obvious repair into a morning\u2019s work.\\n\\nThey began by recording the contacts against a quartz interval counter. The counter had its own faults, but over a few minutes it could distinguish a drifting pendulum from a switch that closed at the wrong point. Ivo attached the leads. Mara opened the inspection shutter 